rade_c's EOO demodulator is off by one symbol against the frame the transmitter sends (P E D D D E) and the
Python reference (radae/dsp.py receiver_one, where Ns is redefined as Ns + 1): it uses the third data symbol
as the closing EOO pilot, and extracts only two of the three data symbols, so the last 60 of the 180 EOO
floats it returns are uninitialised stack memory. That garbage is exactly where FreeDV's text decoder
estimates the noise, so callsigns decoded only some of the time.
--- a/src/rade_ofdm.c
+++ b/src/rade_ofdm.c
@@ -544,7 +544,7 @@
         RADE_COMP sum = rade_czero();
         sum = rade_cadd(sum, rade_cdiv(rx_sym[0][c], ofdm->P[c]));
         sum = rade_cadd(sum, rade_cdiv(rx_sym[1][c], ofdm->Pend[c]));
-        sum = rade_cadd(sum, rade_cdiv(rx_sym[Ns][c], ofdm->Pend[c]));
+        sum = rade_cadd(sum, rade_cdiv(rx_sym[Ns + 1][c], ofdm->Pend[c]));
         float phase_offset = rade_cangle(sum);
 
         /* Correct all symbols */
@@ -555,7 +555,7 @@
 
     /* Extract data symbols (symbols 2 to Ns, i.e., Ns-1 symbols) */
     int out_idx = 0;
-    for (int s = 2; s < Ns; s++) {
+    for (int s = 2; s <= Ns; s++) {
         for (int c = 0; c < Nc; c++) {
             z_hat[out_idx++] = rx_sym[s][c].real;
             z_hat[out_idx++] = rx_sym[s][c].imag;
